  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Together for the Protection and Economic Empowerment of Youth in Thailand/TUI Academy</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Youth in Thailand struggle with a high unemployment rate and an increased risk of (sexual) exploitation. In particular, girls and young women quickly find themselves in a vulnerable position. At the same time, there is a demand for motivated personnel in the growing tourism industry. The TUI Academy has been set up to fill this gap and empower young people. The program combines vocational training with lessons in necessary life skills and an internship at a hotel. Young people thus get a better chance on the job market while developing into strong, confident individuals. The TUI Academy in Thailand was launched in 2020. The first edition of the project wraps up in 2025. The project is a collaboration between Plan International, the TUI Care Foundation, Defense for Children-ECPAT and ROBINSON Khao Lak, commissioned by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs through the Fund Against Child Labor.During the program, 150 young people between the ages of 17 and 24 participate in the TUI Academy. At least sixty percent of the youth enrolled are women. The program consists of vocational training combined with life skills classes and an internship in the tourism industry. The subjects and the internship both last three months. Students can choose from different specialisations, such as waitressing, hotel maintenance or kitchen assistance. In addition, students receive training in important life skills that will help the young people develop into strong individuals full of self-confidence. Among other things, they learn about equal rights and gender equality, leadership and team building, English, computer skills and entrepreneurship. In this way, young people are trained to be able to protect themselves from exploitation and make conscious choices about their future.Within the project, 150 children between the ages of 5 and 17 are also taught life skills at school. The lessons are tailored to their age, and aim to build children&apos;s self-confidence and make them more resilient against exploitation.Social norms and stereotypes about the role of girls and boys in Thai society mean that girls and women are more often kept at home, with no future prospects or financial independence. This makes young women especially vulnerable to (sexual) exploitation and teenage pregnancy. Therefore, the project aims for at least sixty percent of the total number of students to be women. With a TUI Academy diploma in their pocket, the young women are more resilient and have a better chance in the labor market.Both provinces, Phuket and Phang Nga, are a high-risk area for severe forms of child labour and a priority destination of TUI. In September 2022, Thailand&#x2019;s Ministry of Tourism and Sports has joined hands and signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with 20 government agencies and non-governmental organisations (NGOs) to promote Child Safe Tourism Friendly Project. This includes developing measures to prevent human trafficking and all forms of sexual exploitation of children. It has been found that sexual exploitation of children is not a priority of the authorities and other stakeholders in Phang-Nga, while labour trafficking (especially from Myanmar) has received higher attention. Ethnic minorities are vulnerable to various forms of violence, including sexual exploitation. Since tourism in Phang-Nga is largely concentrated in Khao Lak area, the project focuses its activities to increase due diligence processes of tourism companies, multi-stakeholder cooperation and youth advocacy in Takuapa district where workers live and tourism is concentrated.</narrative>
  </description>
